d 0.9 percentage points below the guide given for fiscal Q2 2027. Management expects margin to \n256 What Nvidia and SK Hynix Are Signaling About Micron's Outlook ynix point toward further hikes in memory prices When Nvidia released its fiscal 2027 second-quarter results last month, the company remarked that memory price hikes are here to stay. The se\n285 Zacks Industry Outlook NVIDIA, Texas, STMicroelectronics and Intel in non-AI markets. Gartner expects memory supply to remain tight in 2027 as AI infrastructure continues to increase memory consumption, potentially limiting the pace of growth i\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,re,sys; sys.path.insert(0,\\\".\\\"); import judge_probe as p; c=p.ROOT/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census\\\"; nn=json.loads((c/\\\"numbering.json\\\").read_text()); a=p.readonly(p.DIAG.parent/\\\"items-comparison.sqlite\\\");\nfor num in (40,235,247,256,285,322):\n i=nn[str(num)]; u=a.execute(\\\"select title,text from items where input_id=?\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(num,u[\\\"title\\\"]);\n for l in re.split(r\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\s+\\\", \\\" \\\".join((u[\\\"text\\\"] or \\\"\\\").split())):\n  if re.search(r\\\"DRAM|memory price|memory (supply|cost)\\\",l,re.I): print(\\\" \\\",l[:440]);'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,re,sys; sys.path.insert(0,\\\".\\\"); import judge_probe as p; c=p.ROOT/\\\"docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/census\\\"; nn=json.loads((c/\\\"numbering.json\\\").read_text()); a=p.readonly(p.DIAG.parent/\\\"items-comparison.sqlite\\\");\nfor num in (40,235,247,256,285,322):\n i=nn[str(num)]; u=a.execute(\\\"select title,text from items where input_id=?\\\",(i,)).fetchone(); print(num,u[\\\"title\\\"]);\n for l in re.split(r\\\"(?<=[.\"'!?])'\"\\\\s+\\\", \\\" \\\".join((u[\\\"text\\\"] or \\\"\\\").split())):\n  if re.search(r\\\"DRAM|memory price|memory (supply|cost)\\\",l,re.I): print(\\\" \\\",l[:440]);'\"","aggregated_output":"40 Nvidia Earnings Blow Everyone Away\n  There are margin compression concerns due to memory costs, but right now you're essentially trading about 300 basis points, or three percentage points, of your 75% gross margin in exchange for roughly doubling your revenue growth expectations going forward.\n  Jon Quast: Well, Tyler, I want to just point out what Matt said here regarding the gross margin concerns going forward for Nvidia, there's higher memory cost going into everything with AI, and that is going to impact some of Nvidia's margins.\n  As you pointed out, maybe not a big deal with Nvidia, but let me just latch on to that higher memory cost thing.\n  Not only are memory prices going up generally, but now some of the companies supplying the consumer market have exited that.\n  The memory costs are going to be a higher and higher percentage of the overall cost going into making a personal computer.\n  Data centers, their operators have a lot more room to win the memory price awards.\n235 Nvidia vs. Micron: Which Is the Better Artificial Intelligence (AI) Semiconductor Stock to Own for the Next 5 Years?\n  That order book is a gift to Micron, SK Hynix , and Samsung , the three firms that dominate the advanced DRAM and HBM markets.\n247 How Far Can NVIDIA Stock Fall In A Demand Scare?\n  Memory prices have risen by more than NVIDIA expected, and management calls the pricing conditions extreme.\n256 What Nvidia and SK Hynix Are Signaling About Micron's Outlook\n  After all, the strong demand and tight memory supply have been driving stunning growth in Micron's revenue and earnings in recent quarters.\n  The rapid increase in memory prices has led to a big jump in Micron stock over the past year, and the sustainability of that rally will be put to the test when the company releases results.\n  Nvidia and SK Hynix point toward further hikes in memory prices When Nvidia released its fiscal 2027 second-quarter results last month, the company remarked that memory price hikes are here to stay.\n285 Zacks Industry Outlook NVIDIA, Texas, STMicroelectronics and Intel\n  Supply-chain adjustments, export restrictions and elevated memory prices could also create near-term pressures.\n  Memory, particularly DRAM and HBM, is the primary driver.\n  Memory is again the biggest contributor, with DRAM and NAND benefiting from severe supply tightness and price increases.\n  The AI-driven diversion of memory capacity, along with sharply higher memory prices, is creating supply constraints and component mismatches in non-AI markets.\n  Gartner expects memory supply to remain tight in 2027 as AI infrastructure continues to increase memory consumption, potentially limiting the pace of growth in automotive, industrial and other non-AI applications.\n  On the downside, rising memory prices are expected to weigh on NVIDIA's profitability, with gross margin projected to decline from 75% in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 to 71%-72% in the fourth quarter before recovering to 72%-73% in fiscal 2028 as price increases take effect.\n322 Analysis: What Nvidia’s Massive Supply-Demand Gap Says About AI Mania\n  “Memory scarcity today is being driven in large part by the AI buildout itself, and unlike a component that simply raises our cost with no offset benefit, tighter memory supply is a symptom of the same demand surge that’s driving our own growth,” she said.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"agent_message","text":"**Skip a release comparison. Diagnose the opening misses that the existing rules already cover.**\n\n- **Obligations.** M20 is the strongest case. All three draws kept the possible $125B support and proposed no signing question. A material unsigned commitment can have its own signing question beside an aggregate balance. Old-1’s broader signing question needs checking because it may already cover the same decision. For M12, the source reports an agreed guarantee of up to $105B. D248 permits the programme total to absorb a signed deal within that programme. A separate Ohio amount needs an independently meaningful balance that the annual filing can settle. M27’s source reports a definitive agreement. The signed standard explicitly includes sized, incomplete acquisitions, although v74i says “equity stake.” The $12.9B deal price alone does not establish a separately disclosed year-end obligation. [D248](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:2959) · [Opening standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:278)\n- **Other openings.** M16’s category is already allowed. The standard explicitly lists memory contract prices. M07 also needs no guidance figure to qualify as a main driver. Whether the available evidence supports the specific forecasts is a separate question. M05’s refusal identifies a concrete naming problem. [Opening standard](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html:276) · [Frozen prompt](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/prompts/role-v74i.txt:13)\n- **Arithmetic.** I reproduced the per-draw counts. Under Claude’s assumptions, the two countings give expected net gains of about **1.8 and 2.3** before regressions. Those are scenarios based on three draws and an assumed repair rate. With zero losses, any five of seven gains clear the count threshold. Seven gains can absorb two losses. The evidence supports deferring the release test. [Census arithmetic](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md:96) · [D665](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:9414)\n- **Next step.** Narrow (a) to confirmed omissions. Check the source, the existing question and what would publicly settle the proposed question. Then ask for the model’s account where a distinct, answerable question was missed. Stop the wording work if existing coverage, missing evidence or an unresolvable amount explains the omission. A repeated, identifiable misread earns a small quality test. Expanding coverage to already-listed companies is a legitimate choice for Alex. The frozen list’s granularity is a separate concern.\n\nCONSENSUS: Defer the release comparison, narrow diagnosis to verified misses, and recompute the ceiling before proposing broader company coverage."}}
